{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Answer Leak Self-Check\n\nI checked the problem text for any leaked answers, solution sketches, or formalization notes. None found. Proceeding to solve.\n\n## Solution\n\n**Setup.** We have plots $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$. A set $S$ is *interference-free* if there exists some $c$ with $1 \\le c \\le \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ such that no two elements of $S$ differ by exactly $c$. Then\n\n$$f(n) = \\max_{1 \\le c \\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor} \\alpha(G_c)$$\n\nwhere $G_c$ is the graph on $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ with edges $\\{i,j\\}$ whenever $|i-j|=c$, and $\\alpha$ denotes independence number.\n\n**Key observation.** For fixed $c$, the graph $G_c$ decomposes into paths according to residue classes modulo $c$. Specifically, for each residue $r \\in \\{1,\\ldots,c\\}$ with $r \\le n$, the vertices $r, r+c, r+2c, \\ldots$ (up to $n$) form a path. The independence number of a path on $k$ vertices is $\\lceil k/2 \\rceil$. Therefore:\n\n$$\\alpha(G_c) = \\sum_{r=1}^{c} \\left\\lceil \\frac{k_r}{2} \\right\\rceil$$\n\nwhere $k_r = \\lfloor (n-r)/c \\rfloor + 1$ is the number of vertices in residue class $r$ (taking $k_r = 0$ when $r > n$).\n\n**Computing $f(n)$ for $4 \\le n \\le 20$.** I evaluate $\\alpha(G_c)$ for each valid $c$ and take the maximum.\n\n| $n$ | best $c$ | $\\alpha(G_c)$ | $f(n)$ |\n|-----|----------|---------------|--------|\n| 4 | 1 or 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| 5 | 1 or 2 | 3 | 3 |\n| 6 | 2 | 4 | 4 |\n| 7 | 1, 2, or 3 | 4 | 4 |\n| 8 | 3 | 5 | 5 |\n| 9 | 3 | 6 | 6 |\n| 10 | 2, 3, or 4 | 6 | 6 |\n| 11 | 4 | 7 | 7 |\n| 12 | 4 | 8 | 8 |\n| 13 | 4 or 5 | 8 | 8 |\n| 14 | 5 | 9 | 9 |\n| 15 | 5 | 10 | 10 |\n| 16 | 5 or 6 | 10 | 10 |\n| 17 | 6 | 11 | 11 |\n| 18 | 6 | 12 | 12 |\n| 19 | 6 or 7 | 12 | 12 |\n| 20 | 7 | 13 | 13 |\n\n**Spot-checks of key values:**\n\n- $n=12, c=4$: residue classes $\\{1,5,9\\}, \\{2,6,10\\}, \\{3,7,11\\}, \\{4,8,12\\}$, each a path of length 3, so $\\alpha = 4 \\times 2 = 8$.\n- $n=15, c=5$: classes $\\{1,6,11\\}, \\{2,7,12\\}, \\{3,8,13\\}, \\{4,9,14\\}, \\{5,10,15\\}$, each length 3, so $\\alpha = 5 \\times 2 = 10$.\n- $n=18, c=6$: classes $\\{1,7,13\\}, \\{2,8,14\\}, \\{3,9,15\\}, \\{4,10,16\\}, \\{5,11,17\\}, \\{6,12,18\\}$, each length 3, so $\\alpha = 6 \\times 2 = 12$.\n- $n=20, c=7$: classes $\\{1,8,15\\}, \\{2,9,16\\}, \\{3,10,17\\}, \\{4,11,18\\}, \\{5,12,19\\}, \\{6,13,20\\}$ (length 3 each), $\\{7,14\\}$ (length 2), so $\\alpha = 6 \\times 2 + 1 = 13$.\n\n**Summing:**\n\n$$\\sum_{n=4}^{20} f(n) = 2+3+4+4+5+6+6+7+8+8+9+10+10+11+12+12+13$$\n\n$$= 130$$\n\n## Answer\n\n$$\\boxed{130}$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me analyze this problem carefully.\n\nWe have n plots in a row, numbered 1 to n. We want to select a set S of plots. S is \"interference-free\" if there exists at least one safety distance c (positive integer, c ≤ n/2) such that no two elements of S differ by exactly c.\n\nSo we need: ∃c ∈ {1, 2, ..., ⌊n/2⌋} such that for all s1, s2 ∈ S, |s1 - s2| ≠ c.\n\nf(n) = maximum size of such an S.\n\nLet me think about this. We want to find the largest subset S of {1, ..., n} such that for some c ≤ n/2, no two elements of S differ by exactly c.\n\nSo we pick a c, and then we want the largest subset of {1, ..., n} with no two elements differing by exactly c. Then f(n) = max over c of this.\n\nFor a fixed c, we want the largest subset of {1, ..., n} with no two elements differing by c. \n\nThink of it as a graph: vertices 1..n, edges between i and j if |i-j| = c. This graph is a union of paths. Specifically, the residue classes mod c form paths. For residue r (1 ≤ r ≤ c), the vertices r, r+c, r+2c, ... form a path. The maximum independent set of a path of length m (m vertices) is ⌈m/2⌉.\n\nSo for fixed c, the maximum independent set size = sum over residue classes of ⌈(length of path)/2⌉.\n\nThe residue classes mod c: for r = 1, 2, ..., c, the path is r, r+c, r+2c, ..., up to n. The length of path for resid